{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 18,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.312,
            0.571,
            0.376,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_0.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.046,
            0.364,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.144
          ],
          "content": "When the sum of two given angles is less than $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ } ]$ , a triangle exists with these angles. If the sum is greater than or equal to $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ }$ , there is no triangle with these angles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.143,
            0.845,
            0.181
          ],
          "content": "Let us take two angles, say $6 0 ^ { \\circ }$ and $7 0 ^ { \\circ } ]$ , whose sum is less than $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ }$ Let the included side be 5 cm."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.19,
            0.848,
            0.248
          ],
          "content": "? What could the measure of the third angle be? Does this measure change if the base length is changed to some other value, say 7 cm? Construct and find out."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.267,
            0.848,
            0.327
          ],
          "content": "？ In general, once the two angles are fixed, does the third angle depend on the included sidelength? Try with different pairs of angles and lengths."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.336,
            0.849,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": "The measurements might show that the sidelength has no effect (or a very small effect) on the third angle. With this observation, let us see if we can find the third angle without carrying out the construction and measurement."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.418,
            0.803,
            0.498
          ],
          "content": "? Try experimenting with different triangles to see if there is a relation between any two angles and the third one. To find this relation, what data will you keep track of and how will you organise the data you collect?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.818,
            0.422,
            0.905,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.515,
            0.847,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": "？ Consider a triangle ABC with $\\angle B = 50^{\\circ}$ and $\\angle C = 70^{\\circ}$ . Let us see how we can find $\\angle \\mathrm { A }$ without construction."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.398,
            0.557,
            0.597,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.688,
            0.847,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": "We saw that the notion of parallel lines was useful to determine that"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.707,
            0.582,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": "the sum of any two angles of a triangle is less than $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ } ,$ Parallel lines can be used to find the third angle, ∠BAC as well."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.762,
            0.582,
            0.801
          ],
          "content": "Let us suppose we construct a line XY parallel to BC through vertex A."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.808,
            0.581,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": "We can see new angles being formed here: ∠XAB, and $\\angle \\mathtt { Y A C } ,$ . What are their values?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.598,
            0.711,
            0.842,
            0.845
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.857,
            0.511,
            0.876
          ],
          "content": "Since the line XY is parallel to BC,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.876,
            0.847,
            0.914
          ],
          "content": "$\\angle \\mathrm{XAB} = \\angle \\mathrm{B}$ and $\\angle \\mathrm{YAC} = \\angle \\mathrm{C},$ , because they are alternate angles of the transversals AB and AC."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.938,
            0.194,
            0.954
          ],
          "content": "164"
        },
        {
